161 single-token mutants of `server/jobs_limit.py` were run against the first revision of this file alone, stopping at each mutant's first failing test: comparison, boolean, return, offset, operator swaps, integer-literal swaps including the literals 0 and 1, and the arithmetic swaps `-`/`+`, `*`/`//`, `*`/`-`, `**`/`*`, `%`/`//`. Comment and docstring text is not mutated. 142 were killed, 2 are crash-only and are not counted as kills (`operations[1]` and `operations[2]` in the contract loader raise IndexError at import time, so no test runs), and 17 survived. Fifteen of the survivors were real gaps in forged previous-record validation (the first revision only fuzzed previous records taken from valid chains): the previous `refill_ms`, `cost` and `retry_after_ms` lower bounds, the admit `tokens > capacity - cost` check, the deny retry window lower and upper bounds (`<`, `-`, `*`), and the `updated_at + retry_after_ms <= MAX_NOW` and `now + retry > MAX_NOW` clock edges. Three rows were added: `test_forged_previous_records_with_a_valid_digest_match_the_oracle_field_by_field` (every previous field at 18 edge values with the limit_id recomputed, so only the named defect is present), `test_forged_deny_retry_window_and_clock_sum_match_the_oracle_on_a_full_grid` and `test_deny_clock_overflow_edge_matches_the_oracle_at_every_offset`. Each of those 13 distinct mutants was re-run by hand against these rows and fails an oracle assertion (the 15 survivors map onto 13 distinct source edits, two repeated), giving 157 kills of 161. The 3 remaining survivors are behavior-equivalent: `ensure_ascii=True` to `False` (every digested string is ASCII by grammar), previous `capacity` lower bound 1 to 0 (a record with capacity 0 is still refused because `cost` must lie in 1 through `capacity`), and `tokens < cost` to `<=` in the deny check (the retry window bounds already refuse the equal case).

These are author-run tallies and are not independently certified. A kill here includes a failure raised by an armed object's hook ("caller code ran"); the sweep does not split those from oracle-mismatch and assertion kills. The exact-key predicate mutant `type(k) is str` to `isinstance(k, str)` is not in the mutator's operator set, so `test_plain_str_subclass_keys_with_valid_names_and_values_are_refused_like_the_oracle` replaces every existing str key of the request and of every nested dict with a hook-free `class PlainStr(str)` of the same text and value and requires the oracle's refusal with the same class and code (pure outcome mismatch, not a hook fault).
